# Learning-first Python course rules

- The learner already knows Java, OOP, REST APIs, testing, and backend design. Explain Python differences using accurate Java analogies, not generic beginner lectures.
- When asked about a notebook exercise, provide one small hint before offering a complete solution, unless the learner explicitly requests the solution.
- Explain `asyncio` as cooperative concurrent I/O, not as automatic multithreading or CPU parallelism.
- Treat type hints as static/editor guidance, not runtime validation. Use Pydantic at input/output boundaries.
- Always distinguish mock workflows from real LLM-backed inference. Never claim simulated actions sent email or changed data.
- Default to local-only tools; never add paid API calls, expose secrets, run dangerous shell commands, or change the learning schedule without explanation.
- Keep the seven core notebooks around an estimated 60 minutes; the Gradio/Ollama app is explicitly optional and runs after the core. Do not treat the estimate as a measured guarantee.
- Before suggesting changes, explain their learning objective and the tradeoff. Preserve runnable notebooks and test behavior.
- Validate changes with `uv run pytest -q`, `uv run python scripts/validate_notebooks.py`, `uv run ruff check src tests scripts examples`, and `uv run ruff format --check src tests scripts examples` where relevant.
